<section style="padding:72px 24px 40px;"><div style="max-width:860px;margin:0 auto;"><div class="rich"><p>Fence repair in Dallas, TX covers more than swapping a picket. A contractor's scope runs from resetting one post or replacing a board, through straightening a leaning section, up to rebuilding panels after a storm or rehanging a sagging wood gate. This page walks through each failure mode, what a repair scope normally lists, and when a contractor starts talking about replacement instead.</p><p>White Rock Fence Co is an advertising site for independent Dallas fence contractors. It does not do the work, and every price below is what one firm advertises on its own page, not a quote from this site. If you are weighing a repair against a new run, read <a href="/fence-installation-dallas/">fence installation in Dallas</a> and <a href="/fence-cost-dallas/">what fences cost in Dallas</a> too, then call (214) 555-0137 to describe the job.</p></div></div></section>
<section style="padding:0 24px 64px;"><div style="max-width:1100px;margin:0 auto;">
    <span class="kicker">What We Handle</span>
    <h2 class="section-heading" style="font-size:clamp(22px,3vw,32px);margin:0 0 28px;">What's Included in Every Visit</h2>
    <div class="feat-grid" style="display:grid;grid-template-columns:repeat(3,1fr);gap:20px;">
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Leaning sections</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A leaning run is often a post problem rather than a picket problem. One Dallas firm advertises $300 to $800 per section for straightening 10 to 30 ft.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Broken and rotted posts</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A contractor pulls the post and sets a new one in a concrete footing. One Dallas firm advertises $150 to $400 for a single post, and a Fort Worth firm $180 to $350.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Loose and missing pickets</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Board and picket swaps are the smaller repairs, but the numbers are counted differently: a Fort Worth firm advertises $8 to $15 per picket, a Dallas firm $25 to $60 per board installed. Ask what each quote counts.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Storm and wind damage</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">The National Weather Service counts North Texas storms with 58 mph winds as severe. One DFW firm advertises storm damage repair at $300 to $1,200, quoted by the section.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Sagging wood gates</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">A gate that drags or will not latch may need only hardware. One DFW firm advertises $60 to $150 for a latch or hinges, and a Dallas firm $400 to $1,200 for hardware and a drop.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="background:#fff;border:1px solid #E3DFD8;border-radius:10px;padding:26px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(14,77,110,0.05);">
        <h3 style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:18px;font-weight:800;color:#2A3035;margin:0 0 8px;">Steel posts as an option</h3>
        <p style="font-size:14.5px;line-height:1.7;color:#555;margin:0;">Ask whether steel posts are an option for your run and how they change the quote. This site has no sourced price for them, so get the figure in writing.</p>
      </div>
</div>
